# SECTION A — PHARMACY FEATURES

## CF-01 Smart Order (Assisted Reorder)

**Purpose.** Help a pharmacy rebuild its typical purchase quickly by suggesting what to reorder, from its own data. It is an assistant, never an actor.

**Actors & permissions.** Pharmacy Owner, Manager, Staff (roles holding `order.place`). Accountant and Delivery roles cannot run it.

**Behaviour.**
1. The pharmacy starts a Smart Order run and chooses a scope: low-stock items, frequently purchased items, near-expiry replacements, or all three.
2. The system computes suggestions deterministically from local data only:
   a. **Low stock:** pharmacy inventory items at or below their low-stock threshold (default threshold: 10 units; per-item override allowed).
   b. **Frequently purchased:** products ordered in ≥ 2 distinct past orders, suggested at the rounded average ordered quantity.
   c. **Near-expiry replacement:** pharmacy inventory batches expiring within the platform near-expiry window, suggested at the on-hand quantity of the expiring batch.
3. Each suggestion line shows: product, suggesting rule, suggested quantity, the connected stockists that currently sell it with their price and availability, and the pre-selected cheapest connected stockist.
4. The user edits the run: change quantity, change stockist (connected only), remove lines, add lines manually.
5. Confirming the run **adds the accepted lines to the cart(s)** of the chosen stockist(s). It never places an order. The normal cart → checkout → place-order path (Part 5 F-series) applies unchanged, including MOQ and connection checks.
6. Every run is saved to history: timestamp, scope, suggestions generated, lines accepted, user. History is viewable and a past run can be re-applied to the cart (re-validated against current catalogue/stock).

**Rules & validations.**
- Suggestions must only include products of stockists with an Active connection; products that are inactive, discontinued, or from a Maintenance/Inactive catalogue are excluded.
- A suggestion for a product with no connected seller is shown as "unavailable — no connected stockist sells this" and cannot be accepted.
- The run screen must state visibly: "Suggestions — review before ordering. Nothing is ordered automatically."

**States.** A run is a record, not a workflow: `Completed` (with acceptedLines possibly empty). No other states.

**Notifications.** None.

**Edge cases.**
- E-CF-01a: purchase history empty and inventory empty → run completes with zero suggestions and an explanatory empty state.
- E-CF-01b: suggested product's price changed since last purchase → line shows current price; historical price never used for the new cart line.
- E-CF-01c: two rules suggest the same product → merged into one line, highest suggested quantity, both rule tags shown.

**Local platform equivalence.** The canvas labels this "AI". The platform implements it as the deterministic rule engine above. No machine learning, no external calls, no auto-placement.

## CF-02 Quick Order (Text to Cart)

**Purpose.** Convert a pasted free-text list (as a pharmacist would type into WhatsApp) into cart lines.

**Actors & permissions.** Same as CF-01 (`order.place` roles).

**Behaviour.**
1. The user pastes multi-line text. Each non-empty line is parsed as: product phrase + optional quantity (`Dolo 650 x 20`, `20 Dolo 650`, `Dolo-650, 20`). Missing quantity defaults to the product's MOQ.
2. The parser matches each phrase against the active products of the pharmacy's **connected** stockists (case-insensitive contains-match on name, brand, SKU; ties broken by cheapest price, then alphabetical stockist).
3. The result table separates **Matched** lines (product, stockist, quantity, unit price, line total — quantity and stockist editable) and **Unmatched** lines (original text, with a manual product picker or a discard action).
4. Confirming adds matched lines to the respective carts. Nothing is ordered automatically.

**Rules & validations.** Quantity floor = MOQ; cap = maxQty where configured. Lines resolving to non-sellable products are moved to Unmatched with the reason. The same parser is reused wherever this Part references "text quick-entry" (CF-11, CF-17).

**Notifications.** None.

**Edge cases.** E-CF-02a: wholly unparseable input → all lines Unmatched, none silently dropped. E-CF-02b: duplicate product across lines → merged with summed quantity.

**Local platform equivalence.** Deterministic text parsing; no OCR/NLP service.

## CF-03 Compare Prices

**Purpose.** Compare one product across stockists before buying.

**Actors & permissions.** All pharmacy roles may view; add-to-cart per `order.place`.

**Behaviour.** From catalogue browse, product detail, or marketplace, the user opens a comparison of a product (matched across stockists by name+brand or SKU): one row per selling stockist with connection status, price (only when connection Active — otherwise "Connect to see price"), MOQ, availability band, and the lowest visible price highlighted. Add-to-cart per row (Active connections only). Multiple products can be compared side by side.

**Rules.** Exact stock counts follow Part 7 privacy (availability shown as In stock / Low / Out of stock to non-configured partners). Price visibility strictly follows connection state.

**Notifications.** None. **Edge case.** E-CF-03a: single-seller product → comparison renders with one row, no "lowest" badge.

## CF-04 Marketplace Discovery

**Purpose.** Product-first discovery across all approved stockists (canvas "Marketplace" / seller detail), without breaking the closed-network rule.

**Actors & permissions.** All pharmacy roles view; trade actions per role.

**Behaviour.**
1. A marketplace view lists products across all Approved, non-suspended stockists, searchable and filterable (category, brand, city of stockist).
2. Each row/card shows product identity and the seller. If the viewing pharmacy has an Active connection with the seller: price, MOQ, add-to-cart. Otherwise: no price, and a "Request connection to see price and order" action that opens/creates the connection request (Part 5 E-series).
3. Seller detail opens the stockist's profile page (CF-32 links to the stockist detail page defined in the pharmacy screens plan), including holidays (CF-19) and public catalogue info.

**Rules.** There is **no open ordering**: every trade action requires an Active connection (this preserves Part 19/Part 20's exclusion of a public marketplace while delivering the canvas feature). Suspended stockists never appear.

**Notifications.** Connection requests use the existing E-series notifications.

**Edge cases.** E-CF-04a: pharmacy with zero connections sees the marketplace fully, with every price gated. E-CF-04b: product sold by a stockist that suspends mid-browse → action fails with the standard suspension error.

## CF-05 Customer Sales (POS / "New Sale")

**Purpose.** Let the pharmacy record retail sales to walk-in/phone customers from its own received stock (canvas "Customer Orders", "New Sale"). This is the pharmacy's private retail ledger — it is not platform B2B trade.

**Actors & permissions.** New permission `sale.record`: Owner, Manager, Staff. Viewing sales: those roles plus Accountant.

**Behaviour.**
1. New Sale captures: customer name (required), phone (optional), lines (product from pharmacy inventory, quantity, unit sale price defaulting to MRP), payment mode (Cash / UPI / Credit), optional "home delivery" flag with address.
2. Saving the sale decrements pharmacy inventory using earliest-expiry-first batches, writes inventory movements, and stores the sale with a sale number (`SALE-YYYY-NNNN`).
3. Sales list shows day totals (count, revenue, mode split); sale detail shows lines, batches consumed, payment mode, delivery state.
4. A sale can be **voided** (whole) or **returned** (per line, quantity ≤ sold) with a mandatory reason; stock is restored to the same batches, movements written, and the sale/line marked accordingly.
5. Sales marked for home delivery feed CF-06.

**Rules & validations.**
- A sale can never drive inventory negative; expired/quarantined/recalled batches are never sellable.
- Sales are invisible to stockists and to platform commission logic (CF-22). Platform Admin can see aggregate counts only via reports, not customer identities.
- Credit-mode sales carry an unpaid flag and appear in a simple receivable list until marked collected.

**States.** Sale: `Recorded → Delivered (if home delivery) → Returned/Voided` (Returned may be partial; Voided is terminal and full).

**Notifications.** None (local ledger).

**Edge cases.** E-CF-05a: requested quantity spans multiple batches → split allocation recorded per batch. E-CF-05b: void after partial line return → only the remaining unreturned quantity is restored.

## CF-06 Customer Delivery Areas & Routes (Pharmacy)

**Purpose.** Organise home delivery of customer sales (canvas `ph-areas`, `ph-routes`).

**Actors & permissions.** `sale.record` roles manage; a pharmacy Delivery role sees only its own route work.

**Behaviour.** The pharmacy defines delivery areas (name + PIN codes) and simple routes (name, area, optional assignee). Home-delivery sales are assigned to a route; the route view lists stops in order with customer name/phone/address, each stop markable Delivered or Failed (reason required on failure; failed stops return to the unassigned pool). Address text deep-links to an external map URL.

**Rules.** Route completion never changes sale financials; it only sets delivery state.

**Notifications.** None. **Edge case.** E-CF-06a: deleting a route with open stops returns them to unassigned.

## CF-07 OTC Partnership Programme

**Purpose.** The canvas "OTC Partnership" wizard: a pharmacy applies to a platform programme; the platform reviews.

**Actors & permissions.** Pharmacy Owner applies. Platform Admin (Admin/SuperAdmin) reviews.

**Behaviour.** The wizard prefills business identity, asks for programme inputs (OTC categories sold, monthly OTC volume band, consent to programme terms), and submits an application. Platform Admin sees an applications queue with detail; decides Approve (optional note) or Reject (reason required). The pharmacy sees its application status on its business profile; an Approved application shows a programme badge.

**States.** `Submitted → UnderReview → Approved / Rejected`. Rejected applications may be re-submitted after edit.

**Notifications.** N-301 application submitted → platform admins. N-302 application decided → pharmacy users.

**Edge cases.** E-CF-07a: only one open application per pharmacy at a time. E-CF-07b: suspension of the pharmacy freezes review (decision blocked until reactivation).

## CF-08 Per-Stockist Ledger

**Purpose.** One statement per trading pair (canvas `ph-ledger`): everything the pharmacy owes/paid to a stockist.

**Actors & permissions.** Pharmacy Owner, Manager, Accountant.

**Behaviour.** For a chosen connected (or previously connected) stockist: summary tiles — total purchases (Σ non-void issued invoice totals), total paid (Σ approved payments), credits applied, outstanding — plus a chronological signed statement of entries (invoice issued +, payment approved −, credit note applied −), each linking to its document. Exportable.

**Rules.** The ledger is **derived**; documents are the truth. The stockist-side mirror of this view is the pharmacy detail page (Phase 4 of the plan). Totals must reconcile with the documents at all times.

**Notifications.** None. **Edge case.** E-CF-08a: disconnected pair → ledger still viewable (history is never lost).

## CF-09 Delivery Preferences (Pharmacy)

**Purpose.** Receive-side preferences (canvas "Delivery Preferences").

**Behaviour.** Pharmacy sets: preferred delivery time slots, standing delivery instructions, default receiver name. These are stored as business preferences, editable by Owner/Manager, and are **displayed to the stockist** on order detail and delivery cards.

**Rules.** Preferences are advisory: they never block dispatch or delivery.

**Notifications.** None. **Edge case.** E-CF-09a: changed preferences apply to future deliveries only.

## CF-10 Favourites & Private Supplier Rating

**Purpose.** Pin preferred stockists; keep a private quality note (canvas favourites/ratings).

**Behaviour.** A pharmacy can mark any stockist as favourite (pinned first in directory and marketplace filters) and set a private 1–5 rating with an optional note per supplier.

**Rules.** Ratings and favourites are visible **only to the pharmacy that made them** — they are not reputation data, are never shown to the stockist or other pharmacies, and never affect platform behaviour.

**Notifications.** None.

